A 30-year-old client's plan to retire at 65 on $70,000 a year has a 56% chance of lasting to
age 95 once Social Security is counted. Retiring at 67 and spending $60,000 a year raises
that to 82%.
Client (hypothetical): age 30, $50,000 saved, adding $12,000 a year, planning to retire at
65 and spend $70,000 a year, all in today's dollars. Question: what's the chance the money
lasts to 95, and what would make the plan work?
What I found
As planned, it usually fails: the money lasts to 95 in 31% of 10,000 simulated futures.
Even with steady average returns it runs out at 91, since $70,000 is a 6.3% withdrawal
rate.
Social Security makes it a coin flip: $24,000 a year from age 67 raises the odds to
56%.
Two small changes beat one big one: retiring at 67 alone gives 69% and spending
$60,000 alone gives 72%, but both together reach 82%.
Cutting risk backfires: 40% stocks drops the odds to 38%, and 90% stocks only
reaches 58%.
